WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 99:59:59.999
Phase 8, LLM application engineering. The model is rarely the product. The product is retrieval, context, tools, guardrails, latency, and a way to prove that an answer is better than the last version. This is applied AI engineering. This phase has 3 lessons. Lesson 1: Evaluate AI applications. Build the evaluation set before changing prompts, retrieval, or the model. Measure task quality, groundedness, safety, latency, and cost separately. The question for this lesson: How do you improve an LLM app without vibes?. A fluent answer is not a correct answer. Language models are confident interns, not databases. They will happily invent a citation, misread a table, or agree with a false premise unless the system around them makes that hard. Evaluation is how you stop tuning by anecdote. Write the test set first: representative questions, hard edge cases, and the failures that would actually hurt someone. Then every prompt, retrieval, or model change gets the same exam. You will learn: Representative evals include hard, ambiguous, and high-risk cases., Automated checks need periodic human calibration., Release gates should protect high-severity failures.. Practice: "Create 30 representative prompts with expected facts or a rubric. Score a baseline. Watch out for: Prompt changes without a fixed eval., Uncalibrated LLM-as-judge., Optimizing average score only.. You are ready when: Version eval data., Categorize failures., Set release gates.. Recommended video: Intro to Large Language Models. Lesson 2: RAG and vector search. Retrieval-augmented generation chunks sources, embeds them, retrieves candidates, and places the best evidence in the prompt. Retrieval quality is the ceiling. The question for this lesson: How do you give a model the right context at the right time?. RAG is a pipeline, not a prompt trick. Split documents into useful chunks, embed them, store the vectors, retrieve candidates for a question, rerank them, and give the model only the evidence it needs with citations back to the source. The model cannot ground an answer in a document it never received. Measure retrieval separately: did the right chunk appear in the top few results? Then measure whether the answer used it faithfully and cited it correctly. You will learn: Chunk size and overlap determine what can be retrieved., Hybrid search combines lexical and vector signals., Retrieval recall is a separate metric from answer quality.. Practice: "Build a small RAG over 20 documents. Create 20 questions. Watch out for: Chunks too large to fit or too small to contain the answer., Evaluating only the final answer and never the retrieval., No source citation when facts matter.. You are ready when: Measure retrieval recall., Inspect chunks., Cite sources.. Lesson 3: Agents and tool use. Agents let a model choose tools and observe results in a loop. Tool schemas, step budgets, state, and guardrails decide whether that loop is useful or dangerous. The question for this lesson: When does a model need tools instead of a longer prompt?. A tool call turns a language model into a controller. The model chooses an action, the system executes it, and the result goes back into context. That loop can search, calculate, query a database, or file a ticket. The danger is the loop. Bound the number of steps, validate every tool argument, make side effects idempotent, and log the full trace. A model that can retry forever without a budget is an outage waiting to happen. You will learn: Tool schemas should be narrow, typed, and validated., Step and cost budgets prevent runaway loops., Side-effecting tools need idempotency and human approval.. Practice: "Build one read-only tool agent with a three-step budget. Log every action. Watch out for: Unbounded retries and no cost ceiling., Trusting tool output as instructions., Exposing destructive tools without confirmation.. You are ready when: Define tool contracts., Set budgets., Log and replay traces.. A grounded LLM feature with a retrieval path and an evaluation set. Take your time with each lesson and practice the examples.
